    "content": "\n<h2>The Reality of Variable Network Connectivity</h2>\n<p>In many emerging markets and mobile environments, reliable high-speed 5G connectivity cannot be taken for granted. Users regularly navigate underground transit, intermittent cellular dead zones, or high-latency satellite connections. Modern web apps must function gracefully even when the device is completely disconnected.</p>\n\n<h2>1. The Service Worker Lifecycle and Cache Strategies</h2>\n<p>Service workers operate on an independent thread, acting as a programmable network proxy between the browser and the web. Choosing the right caching strategy per resource type is essential:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Network-First with Cache Fallback:</strong> For dynamic content, navigational HTML, and real-time user feeds where fresh data is preferred but offline accessibility is required.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cache-First:</strong> For version-hashed static assets (JavaScript, CSS, fonts, webp graphics) that never change once built.</li>\n  <li><strong>Stale-While-Revalidate:</strong> Immediately serve cached content for instantaneous visual loading while silently fetching the latest update in the background.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>2. Persistent Local Storage with IndexedDB</h2>\n<p>While Cache Storage holds network responses, <strong>IndexedDB</strong> provides a transactional NoSQL database right in the browser. When a user creates a new record offline, it is written to IndexedDB and marked as pending. When the browser regains connectivity, the Background Sync API reliably synchronizes pending changes with the central cloud server.</p>\n"
